   ],
   "verdict": "The binary min/max shape appears in 9 alphas, not 6. 76/82/87 carry BOTH IndClass and binary min/max, and s12 attributed them to IndClass alone. Consequence: s11's MT5 grouping map unblocks 15 of the 18 IndClass alphas, not 18 -- the other 3 stay blocked on a missing operator."
  },
  "F4_the_paper_never_defines_binary_minmax": {
   "verdict": "Appendix A.1's operator table defines min(x,d)=ts_min(x,d) and max(x,d)=ts_max(x,d) -- window forms only. 9 of the 101 published alphas call max(expr,expr)/min(expr,expr), which under the paper's own definitions parses as a time-series window whose length is an expression. The canonical source is internally inconsistent here, which is why reimplementations diverge at exactly these 9."
  },
  "F5_the_101_alphas_are_TWO_CORPORA": {
   "block_A_1_to_57": {
    "n": 57,
    "with_fractional_windows": 0,
    "with_decay_linear": 3,
